The OSI Model (Open Systems Interconnection Model) is a conceptual framework used to understand how different networking systems communicate with each other.

It divides the communication process into 7 layers, where each layer performs a specific function.

The model was developed by the International Organization for Standardization.

🌐 7 Layers of OSI Model
1️⃣ Physical Layer

Lowest layer of the OSI model

Responsible for transmitting raw bits over a physical medium

Deals with cables, switches, voltage levels

Examples

Ethernet cables

Fiber optic cables

2️⃣ Data Link Layer

Responsible for node-to-node data transfer

Detects and sometimes corrects errors from the physical layer

Examples

MAC Address

Switches

3️⃣ Network Layer

Responsible for routing data between different networks

Determines the best path for data transmission

Example

IP Address

4️⃣ Transport Layer

Ensures reliable data transfer

Responsible for segmentation and flow control

Protocols

TCP

UDP

5️⃣ Session Layer

Manages and controls connections between computers

Starts, maintains, and ends communication sessions

6️⃣ Presentation Layer

Responsible for data formatting, encryption, and compression

Ensures the data is readable by the application layer

7️⃣ Application Layer

Closest layer to the user

Provides network services directly to applications

Examples

HTTP

FTP

SMTP

🧠 Easy Trick to Remember OSI Layers

From Top to Bottom

A – Application
P – Presentation
S – Session
T – Transport
N – Network
D – Data Link
P – Physical

